From 76425557fd06f552de3c5886752a6a59978bdb06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 01:50:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 20/24] Render into and sample ten-bit RGB and BGR

macOS 26 and 27 render panel and icon layers into buffer-backed
RGB10A2Unorm textures and WindowServer surfaces into BGR10A2Unorm
IOSurfaces. Neither was a render target. The refusal is reported once
per attachment; one 27.2 boot reported 207 buffer-texture attachments
(rt_buffer_texture_format fmt=0x5a) and 7 type-11 ones (rt_type11_format
fmt=0x5e), one 26.6.2 boot 192 and 29. The pass that sampled a refused
surface then had nothing to read: the CPU reader declined format 94
(capture_convert_to_rgba) and the draw lost the texture
(texture_resolve_missing).

Both formats are now in render_target_bpp and store_texel_order.
RGB10A2Unorm renders through A2B10G10R10_UNORM_PACK32 and BGR10A2Unorm
through A2R10G10B10_UNORM_PACK32, the same words, so the resident, the
GPU byte-copy Store and the sampler all hold the guest's texel, and a
type-11 surface's resident identity names the format its image is
created in. A2R10G10B10 is optional as a colour attachment in Vulkan, so
the primary and secondary attachment resolution asks the published
device capability and refuses the pass by name (NoColorAttachmentFormat,
mrt_drop_unknown_format) where the host declines it. The GB10 host
reports COLOR_ATTACHMENT and BLEND for both.

The CPU rails read and write the words through one TenBitOrder: the
Load seed expansion, the readback narrowing, the Store converter, and
texel_to_rgba8 for the scanout capture and the type-11 memo rung. The
narrowing keeps the top eight bits and replicates alpha; the widening
replicates the top two bits, so every eight-bit value round-trips. The
reading arm is marked lossy, so no cost floor sends a sampled bind to
it, and narrows_to_unorm8 reports it. The CPU-origin sampled loaders
upload the word natively when the host filters both formats
(NativeUploads::ten_bit). The buffer-texture loader's own ten-bit
narrowing is gone; the shared converter computes the same bytes.

Three engine paths took four bytes a texel to mean eight-bit colour: the
readback narrowing, the leased readback and the CPU seed staging. They
now ask is_four_byte_color, so a ten-bit resident (and an RG16Float or
RG16Uint one, which had the same problem) is narrowed or widened instead
of read as four bytes of colour.

The approach follows steelbrain/reims-vgpu PR 95 (c559ed91) and PR 104
(a4e3ded6, 226fe047) and aquarat 6eff30be19 and d647200cdc, written
again for this tree.

Tests: cargo test --release -p reims-vgpu --no-default-features
--features backend-vulkan,host-window --lib, serially: 2212 passed, 1
failed. The failure is
the_cursor_bound_matches_the_sprite_size_qemu_will_allocate, which
reads vendor/qemu/ui/cursor.c; this worktree does not check out
vendor/qemu, and the test fails the same way on the base. New tests cover
both formats as type-11 render targets, the word layouts in both orders,
CPU reads and writes of known words, the seed/readback/Store round trip,
the narrowed readback, the native sampled upload and the capability
refusal; the admission and readback tests were checked to fail with
their change removed. clippy (toolchain 1.99, Vulkan arm, -D warnings)
reports nothing in the changed lines and 53 lints in unchanged code. The
x86 and Metal clippy arms were not run: their targets are not installed
on this host. No guest was booted with this change.

